{"id":"9a60e80c-078e-40ad-bc1b-ad42a1992072","article_key":"doi:10.1016/j.neuron.2026.10.006","title":"A consensus spinal cord cell-type atlas across mouse, macaque, and human","title_zh":"小鼠、猕猴和人脊髓细胞类型共识图谱","doi":"10.1016/j.neuron.2026.10.006","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neuron.2026.10.006","abstract":"The spinal cord contains cell types critical for motor, sensory, and autonomic function, many of which are implicated in neurological disease and injury. Yet no shared molecular framework links human spinal cord cell types to the animal models used to study them. Here, we present a cross-species atlas integrating single-nucleus gene expression, chromatin accessibility, and spatial transcriptomics from over one million nuclei spanning cervical to sacral segments in human, macaque, and mouse. Consensus clustering resolves 111 cell type groups replicable across all three species, whose spatial distributions map spinal laminae and nuclei, including Clarke's column. Identity and laminar organization are highly conserved, yet abundance and expression are allometrically scaled, with the dorsal horn being the principal site of divergence, concentrated in neurotransmitter receptor genes and including a primate-specific glycine receptor subunit switch. Sequence models uncover a conserved transcription factor grammar and nominate motifs underlying enhancer-adeno-associated virus (AAV) access to a divergent dorsal horn population.","abstract_zh":"脊髓包含对运动、感觉和自主功能至关重要的细胞类型，其中许多与神经系统疾病和损伤相关。然而，目前缺乏将人脊髓细胞类型与用于研究其动物模型相关联的共享分子框架。本研究提出了一个跨物种图谱，整合了人、猕猴和小鼠颈段到骶段超过一百万个细胞核中的单核基因表达、染色质可及性和空间转录组数据。共识聚类识别出111个在三个物种中均可复制的细胞类型组，其空间分布映射了脊髓层和核团，包括克拉克核（Clarke's column）。身份和层状组织高度保守，但丰度和表达呈等比例缩放，背角是分化的主要部位，集中在神经递质受体基因上，并包括一种灵长类特异的甘氨酸受体亚基转换。序列模型揭示了保守的转录因子语法，并提示潜在的增强子-腺相关病毒（AAV）进入分化的背角人群的基序。","snippet":"The spinal cord contains cell types critical for motor, sensory, and autonomic function, many of which are implicated in neurological disease and injury. Yet no shared molecular framework links human spinal cord cell types to the animal models used to study them. Here, we present a cross-species atlas integrating single-nucleus gene expression, chromatin accessibility, and spatial transcriptomics from over one milli…","translated_at":"2026-11-14T08:11:26.352478Z","translation_model":"nvidia/nemotron-3.5-super-vl-120b-a12b","source_category":"doi","article_type":"journal-article","volume":null,"issue":null,"published_at":"2026-11-01T00:00:00","acquired_at":"2026-11-12T23:39:11Z","display_date":"2026-11-12T23:39:11Z","display_date_source":"acquired","online_published_at":null,"print_published_at":"2026-11-01T00:00:00","first_author":"Matthew T. Schmitz","authors_text":"Matthew T. Schmitz, Nelson J. Johansen, Niklas Kempynck, Inkar Kapen, Yuanyuan Fu, Madeleine Hewitt, Stephanie C. Seeman, Emily Kussick, Olivia Gautier, Michael J. Leone, Song-Lin Ding, Yuan Gao, Ashwin Bhandiwad, Jeanelle Ariza, Angela Ayala, Stuard Barta, Jacob A. Blum, Liliana Cano-Gomez, Trangthanh Cardenas, Anish Bhaswanth Chakka, Nasmil Valera Cuevas, Nicholas Donadio, Katherine A. Fancher, Emma D. Thomas, Rebecca Ferrer, Jeff Goldy, Samantha D. Hastings, Daniel Hirschstein, Windy Ho, Cindy Huang, Zoe C. Juneau, Sa Rang Kim, Zachary R. Lewis, Elizabeth Liang, Naomi X. Martin, Josh Nagra, Dakota Newman, Myung-Chul Noh, Paul Olsen, Alana Oyama, Nick Pena, Helen Poldsam, Patrick L. Ray, Melissa Reding, Christine Rimorin, Augustin Ruiz, Nadiya V. Shapovalova, Lyudmila Shulga, Cassandra Sobieski, Amy Torkelson, Morgan Wirthlin, Shenqin Yao, Helen C. Lai, Andreas Pfenning, Allan-Hermann Pool, Rebecca Seal, Bosiljka Tasic, Jonathan T. Ting, Jack Waters, Zizhen Yao, Aaron D. Gitler, Stein Aerts, Delissa McMillen, Lydia Ng, Hongkui Zeng, Cindy T.J. van Velthoven, Tanya L. Daigle, Kimberly A. Smith, Rebecca D. Hodge, Ed S. Lein, Trygve E. Bakken","authors":[{"position":0,"given_name":"Matthew T.","family_name":"Schmitz","full_name":"Matthew T. Schmitz","affiliation":null},{"position":1,"given_name":"